WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.820
What exactly happened after Edward Norton
made The Incredible Hulk with Marvel?

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.160
Why was a respected star kicked out of Hollywood's
most successful franchise?

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.070
From paycheck problems to behind-the-scenes
struggles, here are the real reasons Edward

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.760
Norton was fired from the MCU.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.400
It's widely known that Norton is a guy who
likes to call the shots, even when he's not

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.480
the one in charge.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.180
He showed up on the set of Red Dragon one
day with new lines he'd written for himself

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.220
and Anthony Hopkins.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:31.180
When he refused to star in the remake of The
Italian Job - a film he was contractually

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.980
obligated to do - Paramount had to threaten
a lawsuit before he accepted the role.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:42.420
And perhaps most infamously, he used his A-list
clout to re-edit American History X, a move

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.680
that infuriated director Tony Kaye.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.040
Of course, Marvel Studios has had its fair
share of "control freak" moments, too.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:53.340
When execs didn't like Edgar
Wright's vision for Ant-Man or Patty Jenkins'

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.580
take on Thor: The Dark World, the studio cut
ties with those directors.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.590
Marvel also gave Joss Whedon a hard time during
Avengers: Age of Ultron, with Whedon having

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.340
to fight tooth and nail to keep certain scenes
in the movie.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.120
In other words, Edward Norton is an immovable
object and Marvel Studios is an unstoppable

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:11.620
force.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.920
When the two crashed together, things got
really messy really fast.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.120
Once shooting was finished on The Incredible
Hulk, a civil war broke out between Marvel

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:21.620
and Norton.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.120
After watching the film, studio executives
decided that they wanted more action and more

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.950
excitement in a shorter runtime.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.360
This didn't sit well with Norton, who had
previously been allowed to make his own considerable

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.580
rewrites to the script.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.900
Norton was especially upset because he thought
he was going to have more say in the creative

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:38.540
process.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.960
According to Deadline, the actor had been
"promised tremendous involvement and access"

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:47.940
to the final product, but Marvel wasn't too
keen on Norton's contributions, and they chopped

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.480
out many of the scenes he'd written.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:54.240
Flashback sequences that gave Banner more
depth were tossed by the wayside, and an opening

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.180
scene in which Norton's character tries to
end his own life was left on the cutting room

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:58.680
floor.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.140
While Norton was hoping for a more introspective
film that ran for around 140 minutes, The

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.540
Incredible Hulk became a run-of-the-mill action
flick that was just 112 minutes long.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.540
Norton was upset and frustrated, and subsequently
took a backseat when it came to promoting

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.040
the movie.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.819
In 2010, shortly before the stars of The Avengers
assembled at the San Diego Comic-Con, MCU

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:29.489
godfather Kevin Feige - who had originally
shown interest in having Norton join The Avengers

00:02:29.489 --> 00:02:33.000
- released a statement signaling that the
short-lived Norton era was over.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.620
In the statement, Feige said:

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.629
"Our decision is definitely not based on monetary
factors, but instead rooted in the need for

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:44.720
an actor who embodies the creativity and collaborative
spirit of our other talented cast members."

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:46.280
“I don’t even like the Hulk!

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.120
He’s all like, ‘Smash, smash, smash!’

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.560
I prefer you.”

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.580
Basically, Feige said that Norton was getting
the boot because he wasn't a team player.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.260
According to Deadline, one Marvel insider
described Norton as a "wolf in the hen house,"

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.020
and based on his alleged behavior on films
like Red Dragon and American History X, it's

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.560
easy to see why Marvel might have been eager
to part ways.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.019
Although Feige said money had nothing to do
with Norton's firing from The Avengers, Norton’s

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.860
agent Brian Swardstrom begged to differ.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:18.300
According to Swardstrom, Norton had been looking
forward to working with Joss Whedon and the

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:19.940
other cast members of The Avengers.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.140
Apparently the actor was "enthusiastic" about
starring in such a big blockbuster, but, according

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.860
to Swardstrom, Marvel let him go for purely
financial reasons.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:34.060
Infuriated with Feige, Swardstrom said the
producer's comments were "mean spirited,"

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.960
not to mention "unprofessional, disingenuous,
and clearly defamatory."

00:03:38.569 --> 00:03:43.120
While Swardstrom's rant didn't exactly do
Norton any good, it did raise a few eyebrows

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.200
among the Marvel fanbase - and made some people
question the studio's version of events.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.879
Norton’s own public opinion on the issue
has evolved over the years since he left the

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:52.380
MCU.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:57.840
In 2008, when the Incredible Hulk drama became
public, Norton gave a statement to Entertainment

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.960
Weekly blaming the media for twisting the
facts and blowing everything out of proportion.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.700
But in June 2010, after telling his Facebook
followers that he wouldn't be starring in

00:04:06.709 --> 00:04:08.540
The Avengers, Norton wrote:

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.340
"I sincerely hoped it could happen and be
great for everyone, but it hasn't turned out

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.120
as we all hoped."

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.200
In a 2011 interview with The Independent,
Norton then said that Marvel’s decision

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.250
to let him go had nothing to do with him playing
nice with others.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.460
He instead claimed that it was a flat-out
business decision motivated entirely by money.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.680
Finally, in a 2014 interview with NPR, Norton
changed his tune again.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:32.680
He explained:

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:36.360
"My feeling was that I experimented and experienced
what I wanted to.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:37.640
I really, really enjoyed it."

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.480
But Norton also said he was glad to have parted
ways with the MCU, as being part of the Marvel

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.560
machine would've required years of commitment
and might have caused him to miss out on roles

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.560
in Birdman and Moonrise Kingdom.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.320
“Jiminy Cricket, he flew the coop.”

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:56.220
For a while, it seemed like the war between
Norton and Marvel was over.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:02.420
In July 2018, Norton took part in a Comedy
Central roast of legendary action star Bruce

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:02.920
Willis.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.140
When it was his turn at the mic, Norton poked
and prodded at his Hollywood friend, but he

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.680
also lobbed a few bombs at the studio that
he believed had screwed him over.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.740
Referencing Willis' tough guy filmography,
Norton quipped:

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.150
"I tried to be like you.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.560
I did a big action movie called The Incredible
Hulk.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:18.980
You know what went wrong?

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.500
I wanted a better script."

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:22.760
And he didn’t finish there.

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:23.790
Norton went on to say:

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.670
"I thought we should try to make one Marvel
movie that was as good as the worst Christopher

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.700
Nolan movie, but what the hell was I thinking?"

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:34.120
It just goes to show that, no matter how much
time may have passed, Norton’s still got

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:35.100
a little anger in him.

